1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,883
You'd never expect a golden orb on the seafloor.

2
00:00:02,903 --> 00:00:05,760
But in 2023, NOAA found one, two

3
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:07,625
miles deep. What was it, actually?

4
00:00:07,645 --> 00:00:09,325
That's the first of six strange

5
00:00:09,345 --> 00:00:11,195
things found in the deep sea.

6
00:00:11,215 --> 00:00:12,377
For each one, we'll look at what

7
00:00:12,397 --> 00:00:14,134
was actually found, and give an honest

8
00:00:14,154 --> 00:00:17,364
verdict: solved, best guess, or still unknown.

9
00:00:17,384 --> 00:00:18,980
Number one: the golden orb.

10
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,796
On the thirtieth of August, 2023, NOAA's ship Okeanos

11
00:00:22,816 --> 00:00:25,716
Explorer was exploring a small underwater mountain in the

12
00:00:25,736 --> 00:00:29,005
Gulf of Alaska, using a remotely operated robot.

13
00:00:29,025 --> 00:00:33,138
About 3,300 meters down, on a rocky ledge among

14
00:00:33,158 --> 00:00:36,840
white sponges, the cameras found something nobody expected.

15
00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:40,043
A smooth, gold, dome-shaped object, a little over

16
00:00:40,063 --> 00:00:43,408
10 centimeters across, stuck tightly to the rock.

17
00:00:43,428 --> 00:00:45,390
It had a small hole near its base,

18
00:00:45,410 --> 00:00:48,048
and the inside was the same golden color.

19
00:00:48,068 --> 00:00:49,910
One videographer on board first

20
00:00:49,930 --> 00:00:51,156
called it a yellow hat.

21
00:00:51,176 --> 00:00:53,502
Scientists watching live were stumped. Was it

22
00:00:53,522 --> 00:00:55,950
a dead sponge? Coral? An egg case?

23
00:00:55,970 --> 00:00:57,312
The internet called it the golden

24
00:00:57,332 --> 00:00:58,989
orb, or even the golden egg.

25
00:00:59,009 --> 00:01:00,701
The robot sucked it up with a sampling

26
00:01:00,721 --> 00:01:02,555
tube and brought it to the ship. But

27
00:01:02,575 --> 00:01:04,925
even there, the team could only say one

28
00:01:04,945 --> 00:01:07,278
thing for sure: it was biological.

29
00:01:07,298 --> 00:01:10,460
The expedition coordinator said: it serves as a reminder

30
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:12,956
of how little we know about our own planet.

31
00:01:12,976 --> 00:01:15,156
The orb was sent to NOAA Fisheries'

32
00:01:15,176 --> 00:01:18,677
National Systematics Laboratory, at the Smithsonian's National

33
00:01:18,697 --> 00:01:21,066
Museum of Natural History in Washington.

34
00:01:21,086 --> 00:01:22,954
Scientists looked at its cells under

35
00:01:22,974 --> 00:01:25,219
the microscope, and read its DNA.

36
00:01:25,239 --> 00:01:28,658
They found special stinging cells called spirocysts,

37
00:01:28,678 --> 00:01:30,928
which only one group of animals has.

38
00:01:30,948 --> 00:01:32,611
And the DNA was almost

39
00:01:32,631 --> 00:01:34,926
identical to a known species.

40
00:01:34,946 --> 00:01:37,046
That species had been seen alive before.

41
00:01:37,066 --> 00:01:40,699
In 2016, an Okeanos Explorer expedition near

42
00:01:40,719 --> 00:01:43,770
the Mariana Islands photographed a live one.

43
00:01:43,790 --> 00:01:46,453
And in April 2026, about two and a half

44
00:01:46,473 --> 00:01:49,605
years after it was found, NOAA announced the answer.

45
00:01:49,625 --> 00:01:51,293
The orb was once part of the base of

46
00:01:51,313 --> 00:01:55,733
a giant deep-sea anemone, a species called Relicanthus daphneae.

47
00:01:55,753 --> 00:01:58,398
The part that holds the animal to the rock.

48
00:01:58,418 --> 00:01:59,964
Not an egg. Not an alien. Just

49
00:01:59,984 --> 00:02:02,234
the leftover foot of a sea anemone.

50
00:02:02,254 --> 00:02:05,132
Verdict: solved. It was part of a deep-sea anemone.

51
00:02:05,152 --> 00:02:07,222
Number two: the holes in the seafloor.

52
00:02:07,242 --> 00:02:10,763
In 2022, NOAA explored the Mid-Atlantic Ridge,

53
00:02:10,783 --> 00:02:13,445
a huge underwater mountain range running down

54
00:02:13,465 --> 00:02:15,127
the middle of the Atlantic Ocean.

55
00:02:15,147 --> 00:02:18,199
On three separate dives, the robot's cameras

56
00:02:18,219 --> 00:02:20,518
showed something strange in the sediment.

57
00:02:20,538 --> 00:02:23,258
Lines of holes. Dozens of them, in fairly

58
00:02:23,278 --> 00:02:27,592
straight rows, across an otherwise flat, sandy seafloor.

59
00:02:27,612 --> 00:02:31,382
The first set was about 2,540 meters deep.

60
00:02:31,402 --> 00:02:34,547
NOAA's own words: the holes look almost human made.

61
00:02:34,567 --> 00:02:36,567
But the little piles of sediment around

62
00:02:36,587 --> 00:02:38,899
them suggest something dug them out.

63
00:02:38,919 --> 00:02:41,244
These holes aren't new. Similar holes had been reported

64
00:02:41,264 --> 00:02:44,297
from the region before, as far back as 2004.

65
00:02:44,317 --> 00:02:45,869
No one has seen what makes them. The

66
00:02:45,889 --> 00:02:48,322
robot couldn't peek inside or poke them. On

67
00:02:48,342 --> 00:02:50,378
a later dive, it sucked up sediment from

68
00:02:50,398 --> 00:02:52,731
fresher holes. It gave no clues.

69
00:02:52,751 --> 00:02:55,252
Scientists who studied the earlier holes think

70
00:02:55,272 --> 00:02:57,171
an animal may have dug them, either

71
00:02:57,191 --> 00:02:59,029
living in the sediment, or feeding from

72
00:02:59,049 --> 00:03:01,482
the surface. They call them life traces.

73
00:03:01,502 --> 00:03:03,052
Is it an animal burrowing? Something

74
00:03:03,072 --> 00:03:05,867
feeding as it moves? Nobody knows.

75
00:03:05,887 --> 00:03:08,478
Verdict: still unknown. Something digs them.

76
00:03:08,498 --> 00:03:09,560
We just haven't caught it.

77
00:03:09,580 --> 00:03:11,574
Number three: the real kraken.

78
00:03:11,594 --> 00:03:14,189
For centuries, sailors told stories about

79
00:03:14,209 --> 00:03:17,072
giant sea monsters with long arms.

80
00:03:17,092 --> 00:03:18,843
In Norse legend, the kraken was a

81
00:03:18,863 --> 00:03:21,571
tentacled monster as large as an island,

82
00:03:21,591 --> 00:03:23,178
able to drag ships under.

83
00:03:23,198 --> 00:03:24,540
The legend may have grown from

84
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:26,556
a real animal: the giant squid.

85
00:03:26,576 --> 00:03:29,701
Giant squid can reach around twelve meters long,

86
00:03:29,721 --> 00:03:32,346
counting their long feeding tentacles. And they have

87
00:03:32,366 --> 00:03:35,641
the largest eyes of almost any living animal,

88
00:03:35,661 --> 00:03:38,424
up to at least 27 centimeters across.

89
00:03:38,444 --> 00:03:40,781
For a long time, scientists only knew

90
00:03:40,801 --> 00:03:42,518
them from dead bodies washed up on

91
00:03:42,538 --> 00:03:44,414
beaches or caught in nets.

92
00:03:44,434 --> 00:03:48,212
Then, in 2004, a Japanese team led by Tsunemi

93
00:03:48,232 --> 00:03:51,672
Kubodera lowered a baited camera deep into the Pacific.

94
00:03:51,692 --> 00:03:54,200
And for the first time, they photographed a live

95
00:03:54,220 --> 00:03:56,370
giant squid in its natural home.

96
00:03:56,390 --> 00:04:00,027
In July 2012, a team finally filmed one

97
00:04:00,047 --> 00:04:02,694
alive in the deep, swimming in the dark.

98
00:04:02,714 --> 00:04:04,439
The monster turned out to be real.

99
00:04:04,459 --> 00:04:06,121
Just not as large as an island.

100
00:04:06,141 --> 00:04:08,422
Verdict: solved. The kraken was probably

101
00:04:08,442 --> 00:04:10,576
the giant squid, a real animal.

102
00:04:10,596 --> 00:04:12,691
Number four: the milky seas.

103
00:04:12,711 --> 00:04:16,080
For hundreds of years, sailors described something eerie. At

104
00:04:16,100 --> 00:04:18,632
night, the sea around their ship would glow, a

105
00:04:18,652 --> 00:04:21,614
steady pale light stretching to the horizon.

106
00:04:21,634 --> 00:04:23,380
Jules Verne even wrote about it. In

107
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:25,761
Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the Seas, his

108
00:04:25,781 --> 00:04:28,251
ship sails through a sea of milk.

109
00:04:28,271 --> 00:04:33,278
Between 1915 and 1993, there were 235 recorded sightings,

110
00:04:33,298 --> 00:04:35,886
many of them in the northwestern Indian Ocean.

111
00:04:35,906 --> 00:04:37,806
But nobody could study it properly. You

112
00:04:37,826 --> 00:04:38,843
can't plan to be in the right

113
00:04:38,863 --> 00:04:40,151
place on the right night.

114
00:04:40,171 --> 00:04:43,367
In 1985, a research ship got lucky. It

115
00:04:43,387 --> 00:04:45,755
sailed into a milky sea in the Arabian

116
00:04:45,775 --> 00:04:49,803
Sea, took water samples, and found glowing bacteria.

117
00:04:49,823 --> 00:04:51,028
Then scientists found a new

118
00:04:51,048 --> 00:04:52,930
way to look: from space.

119
00:04:52,950 --> 00:04:56,063
In 2005, Steven Miller and his team matched

120
00:04:56,083 --> 00:04:59,453
satellite images from 1995 with a ship's report.

121
00:04:59,473 --> 00:05:03,773
The glowing patch was about 15,400 square kilometers,

122
00:05:03,793 --> 00:05:05,705
roughly the size of Connecticut.

123
00:05:05,725 --> 00:05:08,901
Newer satellites have since spotted more events, including

124
00:05:08,921 --> 00:05:12,304
one huge glow south of Java in 2009.

125
00:05:12,324 --> 00:05:15,074
Miller's team found a dozen milky seas between

126
00:05:15,094 --> 00:05:19,252
2012 and 2021. But satellites can only see

127
00:05:19,272 --> 00:05:22,248
them on dark nights, because moonlight is hundreds

128
00:05:22,268 --> 00:05:23,930
of times brighter than the glow.

129
00:05:23,950 --> 00:05:26,807
The leading explanation is bacteria. When enough of

130
00:05:26,827 --> 00:05:28,902
them gather together, they all switch on their

131
00:05:28,922 --> 00:05:31,612
light at once. One idea is that they're

132
00:05:31,632 --> 00:05:33,456
feeding on a bloom of algae.

133
00:05:33,476 --> 00:05:36,577
But that 1985 voyage is still the only time

134
00:05:36,597 --> 00:05:39,847
scientists have sampled a milky sea up close.

135
00:05:39,867 --> 00:05:42,907
Verdict: best guess. Probably glowing bacteria, but

136
00:05:42,927 --> 00:05:44,910
we've barely seen it up close.

137
00:05:44,930 --> 00:05:46,763
Number five: dark oxygen.

138
00:05:46,783 --> 00:05:49,529
On Earth, almost all oxygen comes from living

139
00:05:49,549 --> 00:05:52,232
things that use sunlight, like plants and algae.

140
00:05:52,252 --> 00:05:54,127
So in 2024, a study in the

141
00:05:54,147 --> 00:05:56,867
journal Nature Geoscience made a stunning claim.

142
00:05:56,887 --> 00:05:59,737
On the deep seafloor between Mexico and Hawaii, there

143
00:05:59,757 --> 00:06:03,289
are metal lumps the size of potatoes, called nodules.

144
00:06:03,309 --> 00:06:05,977
The team measured oxygen in sealed chambers on the

145
00:06:05,997 --> 00:06:08,454
seafloor. Instead of going down, it went up.

146
00:06:08,474 --> 00:06:10,275
Over two days, it rose to more

147
00:06:10,295 --> 00:06:12,607
than three times the starting level.

148
00:06:12,627 --> 00:06:15,332
They suggested the metal nodules act a bit

149
00:06:15,352 --> 00:06:18,527
like batteries, splitting seawater to make oxygen in

150
00:06:18,547 --> 00:06:20,968
total darkness. They called it dark oxygen.

151
00:06:20,988 --> 00:06:22,463
If it's true, it would change our

152
00:06:22,483 --> 00:06:25,046
understanding of where oxygen comes from.

153
00:06:25,066 --> 00:06:27,067
But other scientists pushed back hard. In December

154
00:06:27,087 --> 00:06:30,261
2025, critics argued in another journal that the

155
00:06:30,281 --> 00:06:34,201
idea is fundamentally at odds with thermodynamics.

156
00:06:34,221 --> 00:06:36,208
They suggested the oxygen might have come

157
00:06:36,228 --> 00:06:39,552
from air trapped inside the measuring equipment.

158
00:06:39,572 --> 00:06:41,848
The original team says they have more than

159
00:06:41,868 --> 00:06:44,463
enough evidence, and they plan to send robots

160
00:06:44,483 --> 00:06:46,577
back to the seafloor to check.

161
00:06:46,597 --> 00:06:48,784
This matters beyond science. Companies want to

162
00:06:48,804 --> 00:06:51,562
mine those same nodules for their metals.

163
00:06:51,582 --> 00:06:54,809
Verdict: still unknown. The claim is disputed,

164
00:06:54,829 --> 00:06:56,441
and new measurements are on the way.

165
00:06:56,461 --> 00:06:58,755
Number six: the giants of the deep.

166
00:06:58,775 --> 00:07:01,200
In the deep sea, many animals grow much

167
00:07:01,220 --> 00:07:04,003
larger than their relatives in shallow water.

168
00:07:04,023 --> 00:07:06,823
There are giant isopods, relatives of the little

169
00:07:06,843 --> 00:07:09,451
pill bugs in your garden, but far bigger.

170
00:07:09,471 --> 00:07:11,709
There's the giant phantom jelly, the giant

171
00:07:11,729 --> 00:07:14,279
sea spider, and squid like the colossal

172
00:07:14,299 --> 00:07:16,868
squid, which can reach around fourteen meters.

173
00:07:16,888 --> 00:07:19,708
Scientists call this deep-sea gigantism.

174
00:07:19,728 --> 00:07:20,678
But why does it happen?

175
00:07:20,698 --> 00:07:23,667
There are several ideas. Cold water slows

176
00:07:23,687 --> 00:07:26,049
animals down, and cold-water animals tend to

177
00:07:26,069 --> 00:07:28,002
grow bigger and live longer.

178
00:07:28,022 --> 00:07:30,579
Colder temperatures may lead to bigger cells

179
00:07:30,599 --> 00:07:32,549
and longer lives, and both can add

180
00:07:32,569 --> 00:07:33,894
up to a larger body.

181
00:07:33,914 --> 00:07:36,376
Food is scarce in the deep, and bigger bodies

182
00:07:36,396 --> 00:07:39,428
can travel farther and survive longer between meals.

183
00:07:39,448 --> 00:07:42,068
There may be fewer predators. And cold water

184
00:07:42,088 --> 00:07:44,758
can hold more oxygen, which large bodies need.

185
00:07:44,778 --> 00:07:46,710
The problem is the deep sea is

186
00:07:46,730 --> 00:07:48,143
very hard to study. Most of it

187
00:07:48,163 --> 00:07:49,713
has never been seen at all.

188
00:07:49,733 --> 00:07:51,565
Verdict: best guess. Several good

189
00:07:51,585 --> 00:07:53,981
ideas, but no single answer.

190
00:07:54,001 --> 00:07:55,559
So, let's check the scoreboard.

191
00:07:55,579 --> 00:07:58,930
The golden orb: solved. Part of a deep-sea anemone.

192
00:07:58,950 --> 00:08:01,414
The holes in the seafloor: still unknown.

193
00:08:01,434 --> 00:08:03,724
The kraken: solved. The giant squid.

194
00:08:03,744 --> 00:08:07,023
The milky seas: best guess. Glowing bacteria.

195
00:08:07,043 --> 00:08:09,094
Dark oxygen: still unknown.

196
00:08:09,114 --> 00:08:11,208
The giants of the deep: best guess.

197
00:08:11,228 --> 00:08:13,860
Two solved, two best guesses, and two we still

198
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:16,430
can't explain. Most of the deep sea has never

199
00:08:16,450 --> 00:08:19,195
been seen, and it still has plenty of secrets.

200
00:08:19,215 --> 00:08:21,565
NOAA images are credited on screen. The 3D

201
00:08:21,585 --> 00:08:24,373
scenes are illustrations based on the sources.

202
00:08:24,393 --> 00:08:25,956
Which one surprised you the most?

203
00:08:25,976 --> 00:08:27,126
Tell me in the comments.
